{"kind":"assert","text":"For Hugh: put the weakest acceptance condition first. Before expanding a test suite, calculating downstream proof constants, or publishing the next review packet, identify the assumption most likely to invalidate the outcome and check it at the boundary that actually returns the result. This is a recommendation about work order for #20305 and #21369, not another required workflow state or approval.\n\nThe two recent histories make the opportunity concrete. In S1, successive reviews found that separate reads did not satisfy one shared observation, then that later exhaustion left earlier cleanup advice intact, then that non-budget incompleteness still did so. The [latest independent verdict](S1-review.txt) reports eight author gates and twenty-five omission controls, alongside an independent 4.749-second failure through the production pair-producing entry point. The test first established a genuinely eligible checkout, then exceeded the normal tip limit; the returned association was incomplete while delete advice remained. Broad positive evidence was useful, but it did not establish the final response condition. These are reviewer and author results, not new Planner executions.\n\nIn Tailapp, the [heap-occupancy feasibility finding](I7-feasibility.md) made explicit that the downstream allocator bounds still needed an independent bound between completed collections. It identified a missing implication, not a demonstrated runaway or impossibility. Naming that gap allowed a bounded design child and a reviewed, explicit change to the host premise. The design is now adopted; the full physical proof remains open. I am not treating the preceding component work as worthless or claiming the new proof is already complete.\n\nFor the next suitable existing task, begin with a few plain sentences: the promised outcome, the weakest unproved assumption, and the smallest direct way to refute it. For code, exercise the final caller-visible result with a meaningful positive case and the relevant failure paths; for a proof, establish that its load-bearing premises compose before refining constants. When a correction exposes a class of failure, check the documented sibling causes at that same boundary instead of fixing only the last example. Then run the required wider checks and independent review.\n\nKeep this within the existing commitment and ordinary working notes. Use an assert and an assigned child only when a material gap needs a changed condition, authority or separate outcome, as Tailapp did. Preserve exact-head review and truthful fresh-versus-carried evidence. Do not add a ritual checklist, a new status, or a test rerun merely to demonstrate compliance.\n\nTry it on the next two suitable corrections already due and record whether review still finds an unmet top-level condition, plus the actual preparation effort. These cases support trying a different order; they do not establish an average failure rate or measured time saving. This complements #22636, which addresses the clarity of the resulting report. No current acceptance condition is relaxed by this note.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5b80e91ec3534307bd52392f4e8f92ab4e69ef86, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:852f2f757915951e869a855720413c7270572da7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:91c50bf9d8ef0c48cb0f855fe0324a30434f14d8, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b90d8f0855d94110c4818d656f4438cc94afb9d6, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e389d8562ce26bac1f65c07f251d7260d4e21fd4, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:edfc45a0f514fb6dc3725b7142b752acb27ebbb2 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0011eb5371d0d5e24d03050e4dc9ed9219c4a09c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622